      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the integer that satisfies the condition of having a remainder of 7 when divided by 15. Let's denote this integer by \\( x \\). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[ x \\equiv 7 \\pmod{15} \\]\n\nThis means that \\( x \\) can be written in the form:\n\n\\[ x = 15k + 7 \\]\n\nfor some integer \\( k \\). Now, we need to find the remainders when \\( x \\) is divided by 3 and by 5. We will calculate each remainder step by step.\n\nFirst, let's find the remainder when \\( x \\) is divided by 3. Using the form of \\( x \\), we have:\n\n\\[ x = 15k + 7 \\]\n\nSince \\( 15k \\) is a multiple of 3, it does not contribute to the remainder when divided by 3. Therefore, the remainder when \\( x \\) is divided by 3 is the same as the remainder of 7 when divided by 3:\n\n\\[ 7 \\div 3 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\]\n\nSo, the remainder when \\( x \\) is divided by 3 is 1.\n\nNext, let's find the remainder when \\( x \\) is divided by 5. Using the form of \\( x \\), we have:\n\n\\[ x = 15k + 7 \\]\n\nSimilarly, \\( 15k \\) is a multiple of 5, so it does not contribute to the remainder when divided by 5. Therefore, the remainder when \\( x \\) is divided by 5 is the same as the remainder of 7 when divided by 5:\n\n\\[ 7 \\div 5 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 2 \\]\n\nSo, the remainder when \\( x \\) is divided by 5 is 2.\n\nNow, we need to find the sum of these remainders:\n\n\\[ 1 + 2 = 3 \\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{3} \\]",
