      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given that an integer \\( n \\) when divided by 15 leaves a remainder of 7, we can express this relationship as:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\nwhere \\( k \\) is some integer.\n\nFirst, let's find \\( n \\mod 3 \\). We substitute \\( n = 15k + 7 \\) into the modulo expression:\n\\[ n \\mod 3 = (15k + 7) \\mod 3 \\]\n\nSince \\( 15 \\equiv 0 \\mod 3 \\), this simplifies to:\n\\[ (15k + 7) \\mod 3 = (0 \\cdot k + 7) \\mod 3 \\]\n\\[ = 7 \\mod 3 \\]\n\nNext, we find the remainder when 7 is divided by 3. The division of 7 by 3 results in:\n\\[ 7 = 3 \\times 2 + 1 \\]\nThus, the remainder is:\n\\[ 7 \\mod 3 = 1 \\]\nTherefore:\n\\[ n \\mod 3 = 1 \\]\n\nNow, let's find \\( n \\mod 5 \\). Again, we substitute \\( n = 15k + 7 \\) into the modulo expression:\n\\[ n \\mod 5 = (15k + 7) \\mod 5 \\]\n\nSince \\( 15 \\equiv 0 \\mod 5 \\), this simplifies to:\n\\[ (15k + 7) \\mod 5 = (0 \\cdot k + 7) \\mod 5 \\]\n\\[ = 7 \\mod 5 \\]\n\nAgain, we find the remainder when 7 is divided by 5. The division of 7 by 5 results in:\n\\[ 7 = 5 \\times 1 + 2 \\]\nThus, the remainder is:\n\\[ 7 \\mod 5 = 2 \\]\nTherefore:\n\\[ n \\mod 5 = 2 \\]\n\nFinally, we need to find the sum of the remainders \\( n \\mod 3 \\) and \\( n \\mod 5 \\):\n\\[ n \\mod 3 + n \\mod 5 = 1 + 2 = 3 \\]\n\nThus, the sum of the remainders when the same integer \\( n \\) is divided by 3 and by 5 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{3} \\]",
